Falsifies `inter_process_communication::verification::a_token_comes_back_exactly_when_its_node_did_not_queue`.

The defect: a receive that collects a sender hands the two tokens back in each other's places.
Both are `Unqueued<T>`, so the compiler accepts either order, and `FromSender(sender, me)` and
`FromSender(me, sender)` differ by a transposition a reviewer reads past.

Expected to fail: the harness's `back == me_ptr` assertion, on a seeded rendezvous holding one
sender, with no pending signal, on the receive branch. In the kernel the receiver would file the
collected sender's token onto its own TCB (`sched::hold_token`) and the sender's waker would be
handed the receiver's, so one thread would hold two tokens for the same node: the double-queue the
token exists to make impossible.

